s:\n27\t\n28\t- `scripts/sentry-read.sh` — the only file being modified. Follow its existing style: `set -euo pipefail`, `: \"${VAR:?...}\"` for required env, `${VAR:-default}` for defaults, `printf 'key=%s\\n' \"$(...)\"` for output, `curl -fsS --max-time 30` with the `Authorization: Bearer ${SENTRY_API_TOKEN}` header.\n29\t- `scripts/sentry-create-tasks.sh` — the repo's established pattern for `tmp_file=\"$(mktemp)\"` + `trap 'rm -f ...' EXIT` cleanup, and for JSON parsing via `python3 -c '...' \"${file}\"` reading the file path from `sys.argv[1]`. Mirror this pattern for the event-fetch temp file and the frame extraction.\n30\t- `CHANGELOG.md` — for the existing entry format.\n31\t- `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/changelog-guide.md` — for the `## Unreleased` section format and prefix rules.\n32\t\n33\tThe behavioral contract is defined in `specs/in-progress/001-sentry-read-event-payload.md` — read the Desired Behavior (DB1–DB5), Failure Modes, and Security sections; they are the requirements below restated.\n34\t</context>\n35\t\n36\t<requirements>\n37\t1. **Accept a bare numeric issue id (DB1).** In `scripts/sentry-read.sh`, replace the id-extraction block (currently the single `issue_id=\"$(printf '%s' \"$issue_id\" | sed -nE 's#.*/issues/([0-9]+)/?.*#\\1#p')\"` command substitution under the comment `# Extract a numeric issue id from a URL like .../issues/1234567890/ or accept a bare id.`) so that:\n38\t   - If the argument matches `^[0-9]+$` it is used unchanged as `issue_id` (this is the bug fix — today a bare `5192501045` falls through the sed and errors out).\n39\t   - Otherwise the existing URL extraction applies unchanged: `printf '%s' \"$issue_id\" | sed -nE 's#.*/issues/([0-9]+)/?.*#\\1#p'`.\n40\t   - If the result is empty (non-numeric, non-URL), keep the existing `echo \"could not extract a numeric Sentry issue id from: $1\" >&2` and `exit 2`.\n41\t   - Keep the empty-argument usage check (`usage: sentry-read.sh <issue-url-or-id>`, `exit 2`) unchanged at the top of the script.\n42\t   - Never interpolate the raw argument into a URL — only the validated numeric id is ever interpolated (Security § Input validation).\n43\t\n44\t   The replacement block must behave exactly like:\n45\t   ```bash\n46\t   if printf '%s' \"$issue_id\" | grep -qE '^[0-9]+$'; then\n47\t     : # bare numeric id, use as-is\n48\t   else\n49\t     issue_id=\"$(printf '%s' \"$issue_id\" | sed -nE 's#.*/issues/([0-9]+)/?.*#\\1#p')\"\n50\t     if [ -z \"$issue_id\" ]; then\n51\t       echo \"could not extract a numeric Sentry issue id from: $1\" >&2\n52\t       exit 2\n53\t     fi\n54\t   fi\n55\t   ```\n56\t\n57\t2. **Emit the metadata block unchanged.** The seven `printf` lines for `short_id`, `status`, `live_event_count`, `first_seen`, `last_seen`, `users_impacted`, `title` stay exactly as they are today, in this exact order, and remain the first and only unconditionally-emitted output. Metadata fetch behavior is unchanged: `curl -fsS --max-time 30` with the Bearer header against `${SENTRY_URL}/api/0/organizations/${SENTRY_ORG}/issues/${issue_id}/`; if it fails, the script exits non-zero (this is the existing, load-bearing `needs_input` stop — do not touch it).\n58\t\n59\t3. **Best-effort fetch of the latest event (DB2, DB4).** Append AFTER the seven metadata `printf` lines, so the metadata block is always emitted first and is never wrapped in a conditional:\n60\t   - Create a temp file for the response body: `event_body=\"$(mktemp)\"`, and add `trap 'rm -f \"${event_body}\"' EXIT` (mirror the `mktemp` + `trap` pattern in `scripts/sentry-create-tasks.sh`).\n61\t   - GET `${SENTRY_URL}/api/0/organizations/${SENTRY_ORG}/issues/${issue_id}/events/latest/` with the same `Authorization: Bearer ${SENTRY_API_TOKEN}` header.\n62\t   - Use `curl -sS --max-time 30 -o \"${event_body}\" -w '%{http_code}'` — do NOT use `-f` (you need the HTTP status to classify failures), and send curl's stderr to `/dev/null` so the best-effort path stays quiet.\n63\t   - Guard the fetch so it cannot fail the run under `set -euo pipefail`: wrap it in `set +e` / `set -e` and capture the curl exit code, e.g.:\n64\t     ```bash\n65\t     set +e\n66\t     event_http=\"$(curl -sS --max-time 30 -o \"${event_body}\" -w '%{http_code}' \\\n67\t       -H \"Authorization: Bearer ${SENTRY_API_TOKEN}\" \\\n68\t       \"${SENTRY_URL}/api/0/organizations/${SENTRY_ORG}/issues/${issue_id}/events/latest/\" 2>/dev/null)\"\n69\t     event_rc=$?\n70\t     set -e\n71\t     ```\n72\t   - Classify the outcome into exactly one `stack_trace unavailable (<reason>)` line, using this decision tree:\n73\t     - curl exit code `28` (timeout) → reason `timeout`\n74\t     - HTTP status `404` → reason `no events`\n75\t     - HTTP status `401` or `403` → reason `auth`\n76\t     - any other non-zero exit or any status other than `200` → reason `fetch failed`\n77\t   - On any of the above, `printf 'stack_trace unavailable (%s)\\n' \"${reason}\"` and continue (the run still exits 0).\n78\t   - On HTTP `200`, proceed to requirement 4.\n79\t\n80\t4. **Extract and emit frames (DB3).** On HTTP `200`, run the event body through python3 using the `python3 -c '...' \"${event_body}\"` / `sys.argv[1]` pattern from `scripts/sentry-create-tasks.sh`, with the exact program below. This program reads ONLY `filename`/`abs_path` basename, `lineno`, and `function` from each frame — never `context`, never the raw JSON (the PII invariant depends on this):\n81\t   ```bash\n82\t   set +e\n83\t   frames=\"$(python3 -c '\n84\t   import json, sys, os\n85\t   try:\n86\t       data = json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))\n87\t       frames = []\n88\t       for entry in data.get(\"entries\", []):\n89\t           if entry.get(\"type\") == \"exception\":\n90\t               values = entry.get(\"data\", {}).get(\"values\", [])\n91\t               if values:\n92\t                   frames = values[0].get(\"stacktrace\", {}).get(\"frames\", [])\n93\t               break\n94\t   except Exception:\n95\t       sys.exit(0)\n96\t   lines = []\n97\t   for frame in frames[:30]:\n98\t       lineno = frame.get(\"lineno\")\n99\t       if not isinstance(lineno, int):\n100\t           continue\n101\t       path = frame.get(\"abs_path\") or frame.get(\"filename\") or \"\"\n102\t       func = frame.get(\"function\") or \"unknown\"\n103\t       lines.append(\"%s:%s in %s\" % (os.path.basename(path), lineno, func))\n104\t   if lines:\n105\t       print(\"stack_trace=%d frames\" % len(lines))\n106\t       print(\"\\n\".join(lines))\n107\t   ' \"${event_body}\")\"\n108\t   frames_rc=$?\n109\t   set -e\n110\t   if [ \"${frames_rc}\" -ne 0 ] || [ -z \"${frames}\" ]; then\n111\t     printf 'stack_trace unavailable (no exception entry)\\n'\n112\t   else\n113\t     printf '%s\\n' \"${frames}\"\n114\t   fi\n115\t   ```\n116\t   - The frame line format is exactly `<file>:<line> in <function>` where `<file>` is the basename of `abs_path` (falling back to `filename`), `<line>` is `lineno`, and `<function>` is the frame's `function` (empty → `unknown`).\n117\t   - The 30-frame cap is the first 30 frames from `stacktrace.frames` AFTER skipping frames without a numeric `lineno`; the header `stack_trace=<N> frames` reports N = the number of emitted frame lines (N ≤ 30).\n118\t   - If the python command exits non-zero OR produces empty output — malformed/truncated JSON, a missing exception entry, or all frames skipped — emit `stack_trace unavailable (no exception entry)` and continue (exit 0). This maps the Failure Modes rows \"malformed/truncated\" and \"missing exception entry\" to this marker.\n119\t\n120\t5. **Update the header comment (DB5).** Update the block of `#` comments at the top of `scripts/sentry-read.sh` to document:\n121\t   - the new `stack_trace=<N> frames` header line followed by `file:line in function` frame lines;\n122\t   - the best-effort `stack_trace unavailable (<reason>)` marker (event fetch failure never fails the run);\n123\t   - the fixed 30-frame cap;\n124\t   - the accepted bare numeric id;\n125\t   - correct the \"single GET\" claim to two GETs (metadata + latest event), noting the second is best-effort.\n126\t   Keep the existing `short_id, status, live_event_count, first_seen, last_seen, users_impacted, title` metadata description and the env contract description.\n127\t\n128\t6. **CHANGELOG.** Add a `## Unreleased` section at the top of `CHANGELOG.md` (below the `# Changelog` preamble, never above it) per `/home/node/.claude/plugins/marketplaces/coding/docs/changelog-guide.md`, with one bullet per logical change, named specifically:\n129\t   - `- feat: ...` for the stack trace emission (script now fetches the latest event and emits up to 30 `file:line in function` frames, with a best-effort `stack_trace unavailable` marker)\n130\t   - `- fix: ...` for the bare numeric issue id acceptance\n131\t   If the section already exists, append to it.\n132\t\n133\t7. **Self-check before finishing.** Re-run `<verification>` and confirm it passes. Then walk each acceptance criterion AC1–AC5 from the spec against the change: metadata block unchanged and first (AC1), frame emission format and header (AC2), bare-id and URL equivalence (AC3), best-effort failure marker with exit 0 and metadata intact (AC4), 30-frame cap and no PII/raw payload (AC5). Confirm your changes are limited to `scripts/sentry-read.sh` and `CHANGELOG.md` (plus `make precommit` auto-formatting if any).\n134\t</requirements>\n135\t\n136\t<constraints>\n137\t- Single bash script at `scripts/sentry-read.sh`; `set -euo pipefail`; `curl` for GETs; `python3` for JSON parsing (both present in the image). No new languages or dependencies.\n138\t- Read-only by construction: only GET requests, no mutations, no shell metacharacters — the argument is validated to `[0-9]+` before any URL interpolation.\n139\t- Env contract unchanged: `SENTRY_API_TOKEN` (required, non-empty → usage exit otherwise), `SENTRY_ORG` (default `bborbe`), `SENTRY_URL` (default `https://bborbe.sentry.io`).\n140\t- The 7 metadata keys, their meaning, and their emission order are frozen: `short_id`, `status`, `live_event_count`, `first_seen`, `last_seen`, `users_impacted`, `title`. Metadata failure must still fail the run (existing behavior), so the agent's `needs_input` stop on script failure stays correct.\n141\t- Usage/id-extraction error keeps exit code 2; the `Bash(scripts/sentry-read.sh:*)` invocation contract is unchanged.\n142\t- Do NOT add a frame-count cap knob, an opt-out flag for the event fetch, or any retry/backoff logic — the cap is fixed at 30 and the fetch is always attempted.\n143\t- Do NOT change the Go collector/controller/agent code, the Config CRD, preflight, or `ALLOWED_TOOLS` — script-only. Do NOT update the agent planning/execution prompt text (a later step of the same vault task, out of scope here).\n144\t- Data privacy invariant: stack trace output is limited to `file:line in function`; context lines and raw payload bodies never appear in stdout.\n145\t- Do NOT commit — dark-factory handles git.\n146\t- Existing tests must still pass.\n147\t</constraints>\n148\t\n149\t<verification>\n150\tRun `bash -n scripts/sentry-read.sh` — must exit 0.\n151\tRun `make precommit` — must exit 0 (the Go suite stays green; no Go code is changed).\n152\t</verification>\n153\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b8442ba12e371c9d","session_id":"5d6cc7de-1074-471b-be5a-1eef1a32f0f2","uuid":"9ea572eb-00fa-453e-871c-24eaa2d7f2cd","timestamp":"2026-08-31T22:35:01.259Z","subagent_type":"dark-factory:prompt-auditor","task_description":"Audit prompt 2"}
