| `make precommit` scope | `Makefile.precommit` | Correct — single Go module at repo root; precommit (ensure/format/generate/test/check/addlicense) does not run shellcheck but keeps Go green as claimed |\n\n## YAGNI Pass (scope-creep detector)\n- [x] No opt-out flag for the very behavior the prompt ships — the constraints (line 142) explicitly reject \"a frame-count cap knob, an opt-out flag for the event fetch, or any retry/backoff logic — the cap is fixed at 30 and the fetch is always attempted\". This is the spec's Non-goal restated, and it is exactly the YAGNI guard the auditor would otherwise demand\n- [x] Every config field / threshold / branch is demanded by the spec — the only classification addition beyond the spec table is the catch-all `fetch failed` reason, which is required for completeness (Sentry 429 / 5xx on `events/latest` must map somewhere; the spec's Security section implies it). Not scope creep\n- [x] No \"future-proofing\" knobs without a named consumer\n\n## Critical Issues\nNone. All required sections present, all code references verified accurate, all spec failure modes and security items addressed across the prompt batch, no container-autonomy / hideGit / cross-repo / vendor violations, and the inlined implementation was executed and matches the sibling test's contract.\n\n## Recommendations\n1. **30-frame cap prose/code mismatch (req 4).** The inline program applies the cap to the RAW frames list first (`for frame in frames[:30]:`), then skips non-numeric `lineno`. The prose says the cap is \"the first 30 frames from `stacktrace.frames` AFTER skipping frames without a numeric `lineno`\", which implies filter-then-cap — a different iteration order. I demonstrated the divergence: with a mixed fixture (some of the first 30 frames lacking `lineno`), the code emits fewer than 30 frames even though numeric frames exist later, while the prose would emit up to 30. Both prompts' fixtures are all-numeric so tests pass either way, and the spec DB3 wording is genuinely ambiguous, but the prompt contradicts its own load-bearing code. Suggest replacing:\n   - Old: \"The 30-frame cap is the first 30 frames from `stacktrace.frames` AFTER skipping frames without a numeric `lineno`; the header `stack_trace=<N> frames` reports N = the number of emitted frame lines (N ≤ 30).\"\n   - New: \"The 30-frame cap takes the first 30 frames from `stacktrace.frames` and then skips any lacking a numeric `lineno` (matching the inline program); the header `stack_trace=<N> frames` reports N = the number of emitted frame lines (N ≤ 30).\"\n\n2. **Prompt 1 ships no behavioral test and does not reference its test sibling.** `<requirements>` changes `scripts/sentry-read.sh` and `<verification>` only checks syntax + Go-suite green; the behavioral verification (AC1–AC5) is entirely delegated to sibling prompt `2-spec-001-sentry-read-event-payload.md`. This is fine per the spec's decomposition, but prompt 1 alone reads as \"changes code with no tests\" (the DoD's test-coverage warning trigger). Suggest adding one line to req 7 or `<verification>`: \"The deterministic AC1–AC5 contract test ships in sibling prompt `2-spec-001-sentry-read-event-payload.md` (same spec batch); this prompt's verification is intentionally syntax + Go-suite only.\" Also consider flagging in the spec that the two prompts should ship as a batch before release (atomic-batch constraints belong in the spec per the global conventions).\n\n3. **Timeout / generic `fetch failed` / malformed-JSON marker branches have no contract test.** Sibling prompt 2 covers `no events`, `auth`, `no exception entry`, the 30-cap, and no-PII, but not the `timeout` (curl exit 28), generic `fetch failed`, or malformed-JSON degradation paths. These are graceful-degradation strings, so severity is low, but they are the exact class of \"marker string crossing the subprocess/stdout boundary\" the boundary-contract rule targets. Suggest adding to the sibling test a hanging stub with a short `--max-time` override (e.g. `--max-time 1`) to exercise the `timeout` branch, and a malformed-JSON fixture for `no exception entry`; if that is deemed too fiddly, note explicitly that these branches are covered only by the operator live check.\n\n4. **Spec-side doc gap (out of prompt scope, for the spec author).** The spec cites `docs/rules/scenario-writing.md` (\"When to Write a Scenario\") and asserts \"existing scenario 001\" exists — `docs/rules/` does not exist in this repo and `scenarios/001-sentry-issue-happy-path.md` is present (so the scenario claim is true, but the cited rules doc is missing). Not a prompt defect; the prompt correctly does not reference it.\n\n## Strengths\n- Load-bearing implementation is inlined verbatim (id-extraction block, curl guard, python frame extractor) and I verified each by execution: bare-id/URL/garbage argument handling and the 40-frame fixture output match the spec DB1/DB3 and sibling prompt 2's assertion regexes exactly.\n- Anchoring is exemplary: req 1 pins the exact existing sed line + comment text and shows old→new, avoiding line-number drift.\n- Cross-prompt contract consistency is real, not just claimed — the marker strings and frame format are identical between prompt 1's code and prompt 2's grep assertions.\n- Constraints are faithfully copied from the spec (agent has no memory), include \"do NOT commit\", and actively YAGNI-guard the cap-knob/opt-out/retry escapes.\n- Verification is strictly container-executable; the operator-only live checks correctly remain in the spec's operator ladder rather than leaking into the prompt.\n\n## Summary\nThis is a high-quality, well-scoped prompt with a complete DoD structure, fully verified code references, and cross-prompt test coverage in its sibling. The two marker branches (`timeout`, `fetch failed`) lack a contract test and req 4's cap-ordering prose contradicts its own inline code, but neither threatens the shipped behavior. Recommended action: apply Recommendation 1's find/replace (prose alignment) and add the sibling cross-reference from Recommendation 2 before approving; no critical blockers.\n\nRelevant files (absolute paths): `/workspace/prompts/1-spec-001-sentry-read-event-payload.md`, `/workspace/prompts/2-spec-001-sentry-read-event-payload.md`, `/workspace/specs/in-progress/001-sentry-read-event-payload.md`, `/workspace/scripts/sentry-read.sh`, `/workspace/scripts/sentry-create-tasks.sh`, `/workspace/.dark-factory.yaml`, `/workspace/Makefile.precommit`, `/workspace/CHANGELOG.md`.\n\n### prompts/2-spec-001-sentry-read-event-payload.md\n\n# Prompt Audit Report: Add a deterministic mock-API test for the Sentry read script\n\n**File**: `/workspace/prompts/2-spec-001-sentry-read-event-payload.md`\n**Score**: 8/10\n**Status**: Good — one localized critical wording bug, otherwise exemplary\n\n## DoD Checklist\n- [x] `<summary>` present and plain-language (6 bullets, observable outcomes; minor format-string jargon — see Recommendations)\n- [x] `<objective>` states end state, not steps (1 sentence, what + why)\n- [x] `<context>` present; references only existing files; correctly states \"no project-level CLAUDE.md\" (verified — no `/workspace/CLAUDE.md`)\n- [x] `<requirements>` numbered and specific (5 steps, exact file paths, command forms)\n- [x] `<constraints>` present, copied from spec (frozen 7-key order, 30-frame cap with \"no cap knob\", no-PII, dummy token `x`, \"Do NOT commit\", existing tests stay green)\n- [x] `<verification>` present with runnable container-executable commands (`bash -n`, `bash scripts/test-sentry-read.sh`, `make precommit`)\n- [x] All paths repo-relative (`scripts/sentry-read.sh`, `scripts/sentry-create-tasks.sh`, `specs/in-progress/001-sentry-read-event-payload.md` — all verified to exist). No `/Users/`, `/home/`, `~/`, or `$HOME` paths anywhere (grep-verified).\n- [x] No bare `git ` commands in `<verification>` or `<requirements>` (grep-verified). Note: `.dark-factory.log` shows `hideGit=true` is active (sourc